 &lt;div class="mlov-rule"&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule-line"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule-text"&gt;§ 1 — Traditional Programming vs Machine Learning&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule-line"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;div class="mlov-vs-grid"&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-vs-item"&gt;
 &lt;strong&gt;Traditional Programming&lt;/strong&gt;
 &lt;span class="mlov-formula"&gt;Data + Rules → Output&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;span class="mlov-desc"&gt;You write the if/else. The computer executes exactly what you tell it.&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-vs-item hl"&gt;
 &lt;strong&gt;Machine Learning&lt;/strong&gt;
 &lt;span class="mlov-formula"&gt;Data + Output → Rules&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;span class="mlov-desc"&gt;You show examples. The computer finds the rules itself.&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-vs-item"&gt;
 &lt;strong&gt;Deep Learning&lt;/strong&gt;
 &lt;span class="mlov-formula"&gt;Raw Data + Output → Complex Rules&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;span class="mlov-desc"&gt;Learns layered representations automatically, end-to-end.&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;div class="mlov-rule"&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule-line"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule-text"&gt;The Three Things Every ML System Needs&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule-line"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;div class="mlov-needs"&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-need"&gt;
 &lt;strong&gt;📊 Data&lt;/strong&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;Examples of inputs and (usually) outputs. The raw material. Garbage in, garbage out.&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-need"&gt;
 &lt;strong&gt;🧮 A Model&lt;/strong&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;A mathematical function that maps inputs to outputs. Its internal parameters are what get learned.&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-need"&gt;
 &lt;strong&gt;📉 A Loss Function&lt;/strong&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;A way to measure how wrong the model is right now — so it knows which direction to improve.&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;

 
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule"&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule-line"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule-text"&gt;§ 2 — The Four Learning Paradigms · click to expand&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-rule-line"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;div class="mlov-paradigm-grid" id="mlov-pgrid"&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-paradigm-card" data-idx="0"&gt;
 &lt;div class="mlov-pnum"&gt;1&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;span class="mlov-picon"&gt;🎓&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;h3&gt;Supervised Learning&lt;/h3&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;Learn from labelled examples. You tell the machine the right answer.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>how google docs lets 100 people type at once (without everything exploding)</title><link>https://shriya5752.github.io/blog/second_post/</link><pubDate>Tue, 17 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://shriya5752.github.io/blog/second_post/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;somewhere in google&amp;rsquo;s infrastructure, right now, thousands of people are typing into the same documents simultaneously. cursors flying, edits overlapping, nobody waiting for anyone else. and somehow — through what looks like pure coordination magic, everyone ends up with the same document.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;it&amp;rsquo;s one of those things that feels obvious until you try to explain how it works. then it gets interesting fast.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;i started pulling on this thread after a perfectly ordinary tuesday afternoon where a friend and i typed over each other in a shared doc and nothing broke. that shouldn&amp;rsquo;t be as hard as it is. here&amp;rsquo;s why it is, and how google solved it.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>understanding shells &amp; commands: because i was lost too (part1)</title><link>https://shriya5752.github.io/blog/first_post1/</link><pubDate>Thu, 05 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://shriya5752.github.io/blog/first_post1/</guid><description>&lt;a href="https://www.notion.so/Understanding-Shells-Commands-Because-I-Was-Lost-Too-2f42da7f7c25805383b2e2068ea1ca0b" target="_blank" class="notion-btn"&gt;
 read on notion ↗
&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;p&gt;look, i&amp;rsquo;ll be honest — i spent way too long just copying and running commands without actually understanding what was happening. everyone around me seemed to know what they were doing in the terminal, and i just&amp;hellip; didn&amp;rsquo;t. i&amp;rsquo;d paste commands, pray they worked, and move on.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;the problem? nobody actually explains what a shell &lt;em&gt;is&lt;/em&gt; before throwing you into it. we all start learning python or java or whatever, and suddenly professors are like &amp;ldquo;yeah just cd into this directory and run this&amp;rdquo; and you&amp;rsquo;re sitting there like&amp;hellip; what&amp;rsquo;s cd? what&amp;rsquo;s a directory? why does this black window exist?&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>